As Boecker and colleagues point out, part of what drives the FOE model involves the fairness process. This is because, in his words, “a large number of studies have shown that people dislike inequality, also known as inequality aversion.” Due to this belief in justice, “fortunes and misfortunes that increase inequality provoke unpleasant emotions, while those that reduce inequality produce pleasant emotions” (p. 58).
After supporting their initial prediction that envy and schadenfreude would be higher in upward comparisons and happiness joy and empathy higher in downward comparisons, in subsequent experiments the authors varied additional factors to examine further. specifically why these emotions occurred and whether the emotions would be translated into behavioral reactions toward the "opponent."
On the positive side of the equation, participants were generally more likely to feel sympathy for other people's misfortunes than to experience schadenfreude, especially when the misfortune was particularly severe. Suggesting that this finding supports the idea of a "likeability bias," the authors also point out that social comparison may yet alter this general dynamic.
How do you become more empathic with others?
When you see someone getting something they'd like to have, like the opportunity to join that outdoor gathering, ask yourself why this is so important. Do you feel that by being invited, these other guests are better than you?
If you feel like your upward social comparison process is kicking into high gear, you can start turning it down to erase your aversion to inequality. Maybe they are being invited for a reason you don't understand.
