Tips for Writing Your Best Psychology Research Paper
When it comes to reading behavioral science research papers, I'm no different than anyone else. I often find myself thinking "what's the point?"
As a teacher and researcher, I regularly provide feedback on student research papers, with the primary goal of helping students better express complex ideas. Based on this experience, here is a sort of checklist of topics to consider when writing a psychology research report.
Very often, I read student papers that sound much more like someone trying to sound smart than someone trying to communicate ideas. You got to college, you're probably pretty smart.
Today, it is common for students to copy and paste content from others' papers into their own. From there, students will often edit, modify, etc.
When it comes to summarizing ideas from previous researchers, the best thing to do is read their work to the point where you really understand it, and then write down your ideas as if you were describing them to a friend or family member. Doing so will ensure your voice gets through.
Sometimes students feel like they have to summarize everything they find in a relevant article related to their research. not so
Now suppose that in your review of previous work on this topic, you find a document that summarizes five different studies that another researcher has done on this topic. Imagine that one of the five studies examined the relationship between general self-esteem and academic self-esteem.
Only include content that is relevant to your point. If there is a part of a previous article that is not related to the point of your paper, do not include it.
Here's something that students often don't know. When very overgrown details about a previous study are included in your paper, it comes across as a rookie's mistake.
In your own research report, describe your sample in detail, but not the details of previous work.
This same rule applies to the statistical findings of previous studies. Suppose Smith and Johnson found that high academic self-esteem correlated with high GPA in a sample of community college students.
Include a brief summary at the end of your introduction that includes clearly stated assumptions.
Summarizing your specific predictions in detail in this section helps provide a bridge between your introduction and your methods sections. Make sure each variable in your study is described in your introduction so the reader knows why it is included.
The introduction did not say anything about this variable. I have no idea what the relevance is here!” Each variable included in the methods section should be described in some detail in the introduction section, especially regarding the main predictions of your study.
One way to help make your presentation easier for your reader is to include subsections in your introduction. APA style allows you to include as many subsections as you like in your introduction.
That said, later in his paper, it's not good practice to refer to his hypotheses by number. For example, in his results section, he can imagine himself saying something like "Hypothesis 1 was supported by a correlation."
For example, if you use a published general self-esteem scale in your study, you must not only provide the full academic citation for this scale, but also provide an example item (e.g., “Here is an example item: 'Overall, I like myself. '») and should describe the measurement system in sufficient detail (for example, are the items on a Likert scale from 1 to 5, anchored with Strongly Disagree and Strongly Agree?
If so, please say so.) This basic process should be used for each measurement instrument included in your study.
In my job as a professor of psychology, I produce psychological research reports and read and discuss many student papers that summarize the research. In my efforts to help students hone their skills in this area, I recently published a book: Own Your Psychology Major (Geher, 2019), which helps guide students through various facets of the field of psychology, including presenting of the research results.
