Many people have only heard the term "collaborative communication" used in the context of company culture and teamwork. It is basically defined as a method of exchanging information that helps people work towards a common goal.
For two people with two completely different minds and two complex personal histories to live in harmony, there needs to be some balance and understanding. Collaborative communication offers a path to do just that by helping people become more aware of all the ways they communicate and guiding them to make an effort to align with the other person in order to achieve a shared understanding.
Our goal is to align our status with theirs, to get a more complete picture of their experience separate from ours. When we do this, we often have to fight our own urges to come out of a more reactive, defensive, or combative place in ourselves.
Specific techniques we can work on to achieve this type of communication with a partner include:
To pause our immediate reactions, especially those that are overly emotional or defensive, we need to give some thought to why certain interactions trigger us. Some of us are annoyed by a colleague's exasperated expression or instructive tone.
Express ourselves in ways that allow our partner to know us and feel us
Communicate feedback in a way that leads to closeness rather than distance.
