Psychoanalytic and psychodynamic therapy: In this type of therapy, the psychoanalyst is in charge of seeking to bring out unconscious conflicts using failed acts, the interpretation of dreams and free association, the latter being a technique used so that the patient expresses all his thoughts in the sessions and emotions without repressing them. This method also evaluates defense mechanisms, which are the wrong ways to resolve a conflict and which can lead to mental and behavioral disorders.
Cognitive behavioral therapy: From this perspective it is understood that beliefs, attitudes and thoughts directly affect emotions, feelings and behavior, with a series of techniques focused on teaching the patient different skills so that they are able to solve problems. It is the most used treatment for some anxiety disorders, such as phobias, but also for problem solving, social skills training, etc.
