People who tolerate clutter can use the "stacks on floors" method to store items that they haven't "processed" yet. Clutter can be piles of dirty clothes or even clean clothes in the bedroom, on a chair, on the floor, or on the bed.
Clutter often accumulates in foundations, attics, and closets from the ground up. People tend to recognize clutter in their homes, they don't enjoy its presence and they are aware that it is not the best way to live.
Braving the entrance to the hoarder's house
Anyone living in the home may no longer be able to easily perform basic daily tasks due to clutter blocking their every move. Bathtubs and showers can serve as storage containers; kitchen counters can be stacked, and even the oven or refrigerator can be transformed into storage containers instead of functional kitchen appliances.
Difficulty parting with possessions, regardless of their real value or condition.
This inability to let go of possessions produces a state of disorder that negatively affects living space and living conditions. Furniture becomes storage for possessions, and rooms become storage; chairs and sofas can be receptacles for possessions, and there may be no way to walk around the house or places to sit.
