Making a difficult decision can be overwhelming and paralyzing for some people. Decisions are difficult because we want a certain result, but we don't know which option will give it to us.
But all this thinking keeps us anxious, distracted, and stuck. In the end, our indecision guarantees that nothing happens, or that we do not have an active role in what happens.
The underlying belief is that it's up to you to find the right option, the one that will ultimately make you happy. If your choice doesn't make you happy, if you end up with the can of beans, then you made a mistake and chose the wrong door.
And furthermore, what if the rightness of your choice is not based on whether you get what you think you want?
What it will be depends on an infinite number of other people, places, things and other unknowns, elements that are not under your control. You cannot figure out the future, because any future you imagine is only one of an infinite number that could unfold.
Growth opportunities Don't ask yourself, "What is the right decision?"
Jenny was asking the wrong question, so I asked her a different one. That is, I asked her to describe the challenges and opportunities that each of her options offered her.
What is the growth advantage offered by your choices?
These are the questions that help us, questions based on what is true and real now and what we know about ourselves rather than what we imagine about the future. Here's the thing; you cannot know what any decision will create in your future.
Far from the land of good and evil, this inquiry moves the dial forward and takes you off your feet. It frees you from indecision and rumination and launches you into clarity and action.
