Despite the enormous effect sleep has on our well-being, there has been surprisingly little research to date exploring how its quality affects our sex lives. Rather, research on sleep and sex has focused primarily on what our bodies do while we sleep.
Given the huge impact sleep has on our moods, physical health, and general well-being, this makes sense. The deeper we sleep, the more able we are to handle the stressors that get in the way of our sex drive in the first place.
Sex is also physically exhausting. Depending on its intensity and duration, it can easily take out a person.
While the night owl couple is up late and possibly in the mood for sex, the other is fast asleep. When the early riser buddy wakes up the next morning ready for action, her night owl buddy is fast asleep.
Poor sleep quality and low sexual satisfaction share many of the same root causes. A good way to protect their quality is to prioritize both in a way that makes sense to you, individually.
