When I retired for good about 10 years ago, the very thought of doing nothing was terrifying and intolerable, a situation to be avoided at all costs.
But 10 years after retirement, I found myself extolling the virtues of doing absolutely nothing, or almost doing at least a little every day. As counterintuitive as it sounds, I've learned that quitting has mental health benefits, even for a short time.
Let's pause so we can define anxiety and explain some practical ways to approach it, at least from this psychologist's point of view. Addressing your anxiety will help you try the practice of doing nothing.
When your worries precede an experience, it's known as anticipatory anxiety. It is likely to cause some physiological effects such as dry mouth, sweating, or forgetfulness, which is actually quite common when you need to perform, such as in a test, while giving a talk, or participating in a sports competition.
Keep in mind that adopting the practice of doing nothing can create some discomfort because it is anathema to the way of life we have embraced for so many decades: relentless jobs, big and small, and the obligations that come with being a responsible member of society. .
Remember that you can stop what you're doing (or not do, so to speak) and resume normal activity any time you want, but wait a few minutes before doing so to see if the discomfort goes away. Stick to your plan and intend to do nothing more than breathe slowly to calm any nervousness.
This may be the first time in your life that you can wander around like a four-year-old with no agenda and just be curious about what you see. The satisfying art of doing nothing is a practice that, once incorporated into your life, has proven mental and physical health benefits such as lowering blood pressure, relaxing skeletal muscles, and sharpening focus without investing in products or services and without any particular effort.
What is child psychology?
Childhood is a time of rapid growth and development of the human being, and the study of these changes is infinitely stimulating.
Child psychology, also called child development, is the study of the psychological processes of children and, specifically, of how these processes differ from those of adults, how they develop from birth to the end of adolescence, and how and why they differ from one child to the next.
A type of specialized knowledge Of particular importance to the specialty of child clinical psychologyIt is an understanding of the basic psychological needs of children and adolescents, and how the family and other social contexts influence the socio-emotional adjustment, cognitive development, behavioral adaptation and the health status of children and adolescents.
Thus, the specialty of child psychology it involves the study, evaluation and treatment of a wide range of interrelated biological, psychological and social problems experienced by children and adolescents.
Stress and coping related to evolutionary change.
What procedures are used in child psychology?
Assessment (eg, cognitive, intellectual, psychological, behavioral testing and evaluation).
Development of prevention programs (intimidation, bullying, addictions, teenage pregnancy, obesity).
Child psychology it is a broad area, covering as we have already seen, how people change as they grow from birth to adolescence and trying to explain how these important changes occur. Because child psychology It is so vast and tries to answer so many questions, researchers and clinicians often develop separately in specific areas.
personality, temperaments and abilities. Psychologists also specialize in different areas of interest: while some focus on supporting children in the school environment (psychopedagogues) others focus on supporting children with atypical development (clinical psychologists).
