But we also go through stages in our intimate relationships.
The purpose of dating is to find out if you are compatible, that there is chemistry, that you have common interests, that your personalities fit. But in the long run, what should be at the top of the list is security: do you feel safe enough to be honest and vulnerable?
Dangers: Rather than risk being vulnerable, you walk on eggshells or are too passive or complacent. Not only does the other person not really get to know you, but you may also end up feeling like you're doing the heavy lifting, and above all, you'll never have a chance to find out if you can feel safe.
Hopefully, at some point, both of you will be more honest; you'll stop walking on eggshells or being so stiff. As oxytocin and sex naturally subside, everyone becomes more real and arguments can escalate.
They begin to build a life together: finish school, establish careers, move house, have children. It's often a multi-year shifting process to move forward, but hopefully, somewhere in your 30s, you create a less disruptive life.
It's about individuation, about becoming more you. Which form it takes depends on the past: you get tired of doing the heavy lifting or your passive partner; you're tired of being micromanaged; the things you've been biting your tongue about for so long are becoming hard to hold back; routines no longer work because they are unbalanced, or because in the process of building your life you have left too much of yourself, you have committed yourself too much.
Dangers: The urge to break up is natural, but you mishandle it. You act out and have affairs, impulsively quit work, or do nothing drastic other than rant or get depressed.
You solve the core problems: stop doing the heavy lifting and create more balance; you survive the adventure and help them both improve their relationship. You have more honest conversations, stop walking on eggshells and build a new and updated lifestyle; you don't get that promotion, but you can reset your priorities and feel fulfilled.
Or not. You are burned; you only have 20 good years left, and it's now or never to do what you want; your feelings have grown too big and you can't find a way around them.
Hopefully, knowing what to expect can help you anticipate and prepare for what's to come. Once it arrives, it's up to you to find your course to move through it.
