Throughout my career as an academic and clinical psychologist, I have often heard that you cannot feel great joy without experiencing deep pain. Sometimes the statement is worded a little differently, but the general sentiment remains the same.
Joy and sadness are not at opposite ends of the same scale. I have no doubt that both are firmly tied to the process that keeps us alive, but they are not organized in some kind of compensation agreement.
That doesn't seem to make sense. We feel good when we get what we want and we feel bad when we can't get what we want.
We can even feel good and bad at the same time depending on what we get or miss. A proud parent and an ambitious career person can simultaneously be elated at their daughter's graduation in aeronautical engineering with first class honours, and also feel desperately unhappy to find out they're being fired.
It's hard to accept that young babies will be able to produce their delightful giggles and gleeful squeals only if they've had generous doses of pain and hurt. Some babies are surely lucky enough to be born into circumstances where they get what they want easily and regularly.
But I don't think so.
Carving out a life of soft, dull emotions might be the answer. Perhaps it is not worth pursuing intense pleasure because then we will not risk being plagued by anguish and misery.
Feeling good and feeling sad are more like the flavors we savor. You don't need to have been hit with the overwhelming, crushing acidity of Umeboshi (Japanese Salt Plum) to adore the divinely transcendent sweetness of Caramel Banana Dumplings.
Make social connections more meaningful
