Can we keep hope alive?
As the day goes on, your brain's desire for sleep increases, and when it reaches a certain level, you need to sleep. But unlike hunger or thirst, your brain can force you to sleep, even if you're in a meeting or behind the wheel of a car.
Why do we feel "fuzzy" when we haven't slept well, for example?
This explains why a tired driver is at risk of causing an accident, for example. If another car pulls up in front of a sleep-deprived driver, it takes longer for his brain to register what he's perceiving, and those extra seconds can be the difference between crashing into the other car and hitting the brakes.
Actually, there is a two-way relationship here. Sleep deprivation can trigger mental health problems like bipolar disorder, depression, and anxiety, and mental health problems can make insomnia or other sleep disorders worse.
The bottom line is that we all need to take sleep seriously. Getting enough sleep (at least 7 hours a night) is essential to leading a healthy lifestyle, and if you have mental health issues, practicing good sleep hygiene is even more important.
So what can you do to improve your sleep and mental health?
Here are three tips:
As part of your routine, you should also avoid eating two to three hours before you go to bed, avoid stressful conversations at night, and avoid using your electronic devices for at least an hour before you want to fall asleep. You can also add self-care practices, such as meditation, journaling, and taking a relaxing bath, as makes sense to you.
Organize your space and keep it tidy
Discussing your sleep problems with a doctor will give you peace of mind and likely help minimize their impact on your life. There are healthy coping tools you can try to rebalance those chemicals in your brain, regulate your emotions, and calm your mood.
