5 great reasons to get divorced
Studies have shown time and again that after a certain point, money doesn't buy happiness (Kahneman & Deaton, 2010). In the United States, there is a positive relationship between household income and happiness, but the relationship levels off once income reaches $75.000, the income level that typically covers the basic needs of a household.
A former hedge fund trader, Sam Polk, calls himself "a former money addict." And for him, the answer is quite simple: his bonus.
Who is going to charge more than me?
Salespeople earn commissions, athletes get bonuses based on their stats, and CEOs get stock options. However, my research shows that this common type of incentive can be a major source of an unhealthy obsession with money (Hur & Nordgren, 2016).
Performance incentives contain an inherent uncertainty: you're just not sure how much you'll earn each month. We show that this uncertainty causes people to focus on their rewards.
We think this finding is important because of an indirect effect: People bring this desire for money home with their paycheck. An increased desire and fixation for money not only affects your behavior at work but also affects your behavior outside of work.
So, it's worth reflecting on your work and life priorities. Have you been obsessed with money since you changed jobs?
Is your paycheck impacting what you want, value, and prioritize in life without realizing it?
