INTRODUCTION Human behaviors can be classified into two large groups: learned actions and unlearned actions.
But the behaviors on which we are going to work are those learned and, therefore, those that work according to the principles of learning. Within the learned behaviors we must include three groups: (1) the behaviors learned by classical conditioning; (2) behaviors learned by operant (or instrumental) conditioning; and (3) the behaviors learned by vicarious learning (the latter are more debatable if they are a separate group).
According to operant conditioning, responses are given to a given stimulus and are maintained or disappear due to its consequences.
Something appears in the middle after emitting behavior that is pleasant, which is what we expected. The appearance of this stimulus leads to the increase or establishment of a behavior.
The extrinsic reinforcer is so named because its reinforcing value is given to it by the external environment. For example, being praised for getting good grades is an extrinsic reinforcer.
Secondary reinforcers are all other reinforcers: learned ones. Secondary reinforcers are learned through two processes: (1) their relationship with the primary ones (eg, with money we can buy food, have security, heat, etc.
The Premack principle states that given two activities that a person is doing voluntarily, the more frequent activity can serve as a reinforcer of the less frequent, if they are made contingent. Eg the child prefers to eat the dessert to eat the soup, so we know that the dessert can serve as a reinforcer to eat the soup: “if you eat the soup, I will let you eat the dessert”.
When choosing the reinforcers to be associated with a behavior, several aspects must be taken into account:
- The important thing is that it goes from external to internal control (internally reinforcers). For that, the material reinforcers must always be accompanied by the social ones (so that they acquire more and more value).
For the reinforcer to be effective it must meet these conditions:
Contingency: In other words, the reinforcer should be granted if and only if the behavior occurs. If it appeared without the behavior, the behavior would cease to occur and if it does not occur when the behavior occurs, it will not carry it out either.
Specification of reinforcement: Every person will act better if he is very clear from the beginning: (1) the behavior to be emitted; (2) the conditions under which it must be issued; (3) the contingencies it holds with respect to reinforcement.
The booster is given after a variable time and not at fixed intervals as in the previous case. In this way, the subject never knows when the next reinforcer will arrive.
