Reflection involves "taking a step back" before making a decision to consider how much information you really have and how confident you need to be about the best course of action. If you don't have enough information, you can find ways to increase your confidence level before committing to a choice.
One option would be to "go with what you know" and go for the laptop-shaped case. The other option would be to stop and reflect on the fact that both boxes could still fit in the laptop, and then come up with a plan to get more information.
This two-step process of reflecting on your own uncertainty and then finding ways to learn more will often pay off by increasing your chances of getting what you want. Conversely, inadvertently “jumping to conclusions” and making a decision before taking the time to reflect can have its costs.
In the lab, reflection is sometimes measured simply by asking people to rate how strongly they agree with statements like, "I like to gather a lot of different kinds of evidence before I decide what to do." Other approaches ask people questions designed to have intuitive answers that are wrong.
