Fourteen-year-old John is filled with dread when he misses the shot in the fourth quarter of the basketball game. As he walks toward the bench, he sees his father's face in the stands and knows he's waiting for him.
His wife is especially upset when she notices that John is harsh with his children. John is critical and demanding in his requests, and his usual response to his deflated boys is "If you don't like it, too bad."
Paul knows that he has provided himself and his family with a good home and a good life, but still, he feels that something is missing. Exhausted by the demands of his job and his family, although he enjoys helping others, he often feels overwhelmed, empty, and alone.
While emotional abuse and emotional neglect can affect you in different ways, there are similarities between John and Paul that stem from their emotional treatment in childhood. Both men are out of touch with their feelings and feel confused and lost in multiple areas of their lives.
If you experienced both emotional abuse and emotional neglect, work on the emotional abuse first. It is easier to identify actions taken against you (emotional abuse) rather than subtle and often unseen moments when your needs are not being met (emotional neglect).
If you recognize signs of childhood emotional neglect, learn as much as you can about emotional neglect, how it happens, and the mark it leaves on you. Therapy can also be helpful.
Know that you can recover from the negative effects of emotional abuse or emotional neglect. It's time to start treating yourself well with regular attention to your own needs and gentle, loving self-care.
