Time to rethink the hedonic treadmill
Your happiness may increase, but it's likely to level off over time. Job loss?
When first introduced, the idea of a hedonic treadmill was a bit of a disappointment to those of us looking to improve our happiness.
Fortunately, we are now beginning to have a more nuanced understanding of the hedonic treadmill, and I propose that it is time to ditch this worn-out term in favor of something more empowering.
The concept of a treadmill downplays how important our autonomy and agency are to our ability to create joy. Instead, it frames happiness as a function of what happens to us.
(Some psychologists even argue that acceptance of death is the ultimate goal of life.) Second, it implies that life is changeless, and that we are essentially powerless to change our circumstances and, more importantly, our responses to our circumstances, even though we now understand that variable hedonics is one of the best ways to circumvent our fixed point of happiness.
Furthermore, as we grow and evolve throughout our lives in reaction to our circumstances, we can become aware of the treadmill effect and train ourselves to circumvent it. A more powerful metaphor
For example, we know that many can increase happiness through exercise, meditation, spending time with friends, and deliberately cultivating fun. As you accumulate life experience and mature, you learn to steer yourself into more pleasant waters with greater reliability.
We have all experienced this, whether at home or at work. Perhaps when your partner is in a good mood, you feel more well-being.
