Other employees are likely to stay as well, because we like to work with people who have developed interpersonal skills. It makes them great collaborators and good team players; they are reliable, responsible, and courteous, and they make work easier and more successful for everyone.
Hundreds of Oregon employers surveyed for the 2022 Oregon Talent Assessment suggested that schools may want to offer soft skills training to help prospective employees prepare for the changing job market.
And yet, unlike coding, civil engineering, architecture, or law, there are few formal classes or programs focused on developing these essential qualities. So what do we do?
The very skills employers want to showcase in their operations are the exact same things we need to be successful in our families, schools, and neighborhoods. They are how we get along with each other, prevail and get the job done despite the odds.
In our lives, whether we're dropping off dry cleaning or picking up the kid from school, buying a pizza, or waiting at a bus stop, good soft skills are about building connections and validating others. In a world where so many feel untethered, alone, and isolated, a moment of eye contact, kindness, compassion, a quick hello, or other courtesy can help people feel seen and keep them coming back.
In any of those situations, your patience, communication, attitude and other skills will lead to something powerful and successful, something that makes people feel better, more generous and helpful, or something that creates the exact opposite and repels customers, friends, and the people you want to connect with.
Consider the feedback you've received from friends and coworkers, consider past successes, and then identify your strengths. Then do just the opposite.
Pick a couple of those interpersonal skills you'd like to improve and pay attention. Who does a good job of demonstrating exactly what you want to improve?
. Soft skills are what every employer wants and every family needs.
