In short, there is a bit of the scientist, poet, artist, and believer in almost all of us. In a world that often works so hard to paint people as "us or them," we share much more with others than we normally appreciate, and seeing that commonality is important for everything from developing empathy for others ( Batson, 1991) to reducing prejudice (Todd & Galinsky, 2014).
Awe is the sense of wonder inspired by being in the presence of overwhelming and vast experiences that transcend our everyday experiences. People often experience awe when visiting the Grand Canyon, worshiping in a grand cathedral, or gazing out into the cosmos.
So realizing that Earth is just a speck of dust in our galaxy and that the Webb Telescope can show us hundreds of galaxies in a single image that we've never seen before until this week reminds everyone that we ourselves, our egos, our goals and our achievements are so incredibly small in the grand scheme of the universe. And to the extent that people feel "less big," they are less likely to indulge in entitlement and narcissism (Morf & Rhodewalt, 2001; Piff, 2014).
Finally, beyond the inspiration and awe caused by the Webb Telescope, another notable feature of the project is that it has been developed, built, and operated by a collection of scientists from NASA, the European Space Agency, and the Canadian Space Agency. Thus, the Webb Telescope is not just "an American triumph," but a collective effort involving more than 1000 scientists from more than a dozen countries around the world.
However, all the pieces and equipment worked together to make the Webb Telescope a reality. This project is an amazing example of cooperation and interconnectedness, which should give us hope that we can solve many problems here on earth with similar commitment, common purpose, and successful results.
