It's almost impossible to get bored these days.
An endless amount of information, movies, social connection and games are at our fingertips. What more can we ask for?
It arises from our very human desire not to get bored. In fact, one study found that 67 percent of men and 25 percent of women would rather give themselves a small electric shock than be left alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes.
Is boredom necessary for creative work?
Instead of being inspired by loneliness, I felt completely devoid of creativity. My restless mind demanded entertainment, instead of sustained focus.
If we want to achieve focus, as well as a consistent creative vision, we need to work on something counterintuitive: our ability to get bored.
Just as our muscles need rest days between workouts to get stronger, our minds need periods of downtime to process the world around us. We do it at night while we dream; during the day, our minds tend to process through daydreaming.
You've probably experienced it during times of boredom when you start imagining plans for a backyard remodeling project or have fantasies of uprooting yourself from your current job to pursue your dream position.
As I brought up during my own writing retreat years ago, the ability to be bored and the ability to be creative go hand in hand. Looking at the science shows there could be something to that.
Boredom naturally fosters the fundamental facet of wonder, openness. An early openness to new experiences and one's environment leads to more potential creative vision.
Take advantage of those periods of boredom that are integrated into your trip, rest time or meals. Turn off the podcast, stop scrolling through the news, and let your mind lie fallow so the seeds of your next idea have room to grow.
But to truly be an informed citizen, you must allow time for idleness. Instead of seeking stimulation by looking for the next event to be entertained or enraged, boredom gives you space to think about the information you're gathering and start challenging ideas.
The next time you feel compelled to reach for your phone, I dare you to ask what you're looking for.
Creative stimulus?
Connection with the world?
When you cultivate a deliberate practice of boredom, I think you'll be amazed at the places your mind can take you.
