5 steps to better understand your bipolar disorder
Many people who are new to a diagnosis of bipolar disorder are interested in understanding what it means to have bipolar disorder and how to manage it.
The most critical step in understanding your bipolar disorder is understanding your bipolar disorder. Your experience of bipolar disorder (such as the symptoms that most appeal to you) may be different from someone else's experience of bipolar disorder.
Familiarize yourself with the symptoms of depression and (hypo)mania, and other symptoms that may accompany your bipolar disorder (such as anxiety and substance use).
Does your (hypo)mania make you feel more productive than usual, or does it lead you to start several tasks but not finish any of them?
In general, research suggests that several factors in combination can contribute to someone developing bipolar disorder. Some of these factors may include genes (such as having a family history of bipolar disorder) and stressful life events (these may be events that cause negative or positive changes in your life, such as changes in a significant relationship or starting a new job) .
When learning about bipolar disorder, turn to credible resources backed by science for accurate information. The websites of organizations like the National Institute of Mental Health (an organization dedicated to research on mental illness) or the Depression and Bipolar Support Alliance (an organization that provides support for people with depression and bipolar disorder) are good free places to start .
Be aware of your own personal triggers for mood symptoms
Your personal warning signs give you a clue that a depressive or (hypo)manic episode is beginning to develop. Take note of your own personal warning signs for mood episodes.
Consider any behavior you observe along with changes in your mood; these can also be your personal warning signs. For example, you may notice that when you start to feel the first symptoms of (hypo)mania, such as a mild increase in energy, you notice increases in your spending patterns that are not typical for you (a possible warning sign of (hypo)mania).
