Other Circumcision Myths You May Believe: Hygiene and Sexually Transmitted Diseases
This is part 2 of our series on circumcision myths.
Myth: Young boys don't clean under the foreskin and get infections.
Reality check: In fact, smegma is produced by the genitalia of both women and men during their childbearing years. Smegma is made up of sebum and skin cells and lubricates the foreskin and glans in men, as well as the clitoral hood and inner labia in women.
Reality check: Medical advice may have favored infection in uncircumcised men. An alarming number of doctors are unaware of normal foreskin development, and they (wrongly) tell parents to retract the baby's foreskin and wash it inside every diaper change.
Myth: My son was diagnosed with phimosis and therefore had to be circumcised. Reality check: Phimosis means that the foreskin does not retract.
Even some adult males have a foreskin that doesn't retract, but as long as it doesn't interfere with sexual intercourse, that's fine, as urination itself cleanses the inside of the foreskin.
In Africa, where recent studies have been done, HIV transmission occurs primarily through sexual intercourse between men and women, but in the United States it is transmitted primarily through blood exposure (such as sharing needles) and through of sexual relations between men. Male circumcision does not protect women against HIV or men who have sex with men (Wawer 2009, Jameson 2009).
In the United States, during the AIDS epidemic of the 1980s and 1990s, about 85% of adult men were circumcised (circumcision rates much higher than in Africa), but HIV was still spreading.
Myth: Circumcision is worth it because it can save lives.
Psychological harm from circumcision
