Many people with eating disorders struggle with what is known as anosognosia; basically, they can't see how sick they really are. That, coupled with a society riddled with fat bias and a dietary culture, can make it easy for people to feel ambivalent about the recovery process.
Ask yourself what your life will look like in five years if you remain stuck with an eating disorder compared to what it will look like in five years if you make a full recovery. Be descriptive and detailed; writing it down can be helpful.
Explore with a therapist the factors that are strengthening the part of you that may be attracted to the eating disorder, and see how you can decrease the ones in your control (i.e., find healthier ways to meet your needs). Explore your true values vs.
Make a list of the ways the eating disorder can take you away from your true values. What is the cost to your life if you get stuck in an eating disorder?
Reflect on what prompted you to want to recover from your eating disorder in the first place, and work to connect with and strengthen that part of you.
