When is it psychologically healthy to put yourself in uncomfortable, even risky, situations, and when can it be harmful?
Some recent research provides new answers.
The researchers noted that feelings of discomfort or fear can become barriers to new growth and success. But such emotions necessarily have to exist before you can experience any progress or success.
So, this study explored the positive potential of embracing and accepting those disturbing, otherwise inhibiting emotions; “possess” them as part of your total being, your complete emotional reality. So instead of resigning yourself to those inhibitions or fears as a necessary detour, if you see them as valuable motivation and energy you need to reach your goal, what impact might that have?
The upshot, according to the researchers, is that seeking out feelings of discomfort can be a source of positive motivation, and in a variety of situations. That is, if you can see the discomfort as a sign of an initial move toward what you hope or want to achieve, it can motivate you to take action, rather than retreat and assume you've hit a dead end.
First, the theme of “acceptance” regarding your emotional reality is a fundamental foundation for healing old conflicts and developing greater psychological health, either through psychotherapy or through everyday life experience. But with perspective and judgment on a new situation or challenge you'd like to take on, it's important to distinguish when discomfort is worth using as motivation to pursue something, and when it's wise to let it go.
Both are important; both are ways of interrupting your karma (either by thinking of your pattern in the traditional Buddhist sense or as a metaphor for a habitual path in your life). That is, embracing your emotional reality includes knowing when it's not helpful to keep trying in the same direction when you've made repeated efforts and the pain keeps piling up, rather than signs of progress, as in the previous study.
