From the physician's perspective, the psychiatric consultation is key to gathering as much information as possible in order to diagnose the problem, determine the best treatment, and outline what you can expect to feel and when during the treatment process.
However, if someone were to overhear me conversing with a new patient at the beginning of a session, it might sound like we were just having a casual conversation: "How are you feeling?"
“Has anyone in your family suffered from a similar problem?”
It is an information gathering tool commonly known in the medical community by the SOAP acronym for Subjective, Objective, Assessment, and Plan.
You hold the clues to what will eventually be the solving of the medical mystery: your diagnosis. The more you share about your symptoms, how they affect your life, and your family, medical, and medication history, the easier it will be for your health care provider to determine your diagnosis.
Whichever consultation format your health care provider uses, be sure to bring notes about what's been bothering you and the results of recent health tests to your appointment, so your doctor has the information needed to make a diagnosis. accurate.
Tone of voice, appearance, posture, behavior, attitude, hygiene, grooming, emotional expression, rhythm and patterns of speech, eye contact or lack of eye contact, and thought processes they contain clues about a person's state of mind.
Your doctor may also request that you send reports of your blood work and physical exam, as laboratory findings and screening tests may influence the treatment chosen.
Depression and anxiety are related, and many people with depression may find their mood more anxious than depressed. There is also a strong cognitive component to depression.
In addition to cognitive and mood symptoms, there are somatic signs of depression, including trouble eating, sleeping, sexual interest, and body aches. When I explain to patients that their constellation of symptoms leads me to believe they are suffering from depression, some are surprised.
I don't cry all day. I'm just anxious," is a typical response.
Often there is also relief. Ultimately, my patients understand that seemingly unrelated physical, emotional, and cognitive symptoms are tied to one condition.
Plan: outline the path to recovery
Anxiety is relieved when an appropriate and reassuring treatment plan is established. After a psychiatric consultation, you should feel that you are embarking on a journey and that you have been given good instructions, that you are prepared for the signs and detours that you may encounter, and that you feel confident that you will get where you want to go.
Some differences in sensory processing are normal; after all, no two people experience the world in exactly the same way. I would like to keep the heating in my house on full blast while my friend prefers the cold.
Sensory differences only reach the level of a disorder when they interfere with daily life and limit a child's ability to thrive in their environment. In these cases, intervention can improve learning, development, and general well-being.
Your child is easily overwhelmed in colorful or noisy places like the playground or the supermarket.
Your child is easily startled by sudden noise, touch, or movement
Your child has difficulty with potty training
Your child bites their nails, chews their hair, or sucks on their clothes
Your child has trouble reading, especially out loud
Your child can't tell the difference between similar objects, such as a dime and a quarter, by touch
Your child has trouble doing fine motor tasks, like using crayons or writing.
How is sensory processing disorder diagnosed?
Diagnosing SPD can be challenging because there is disagreement in the medical community about whether it should be recognized as a stand-alone disorder or simply as a set of symptoms that commonly co-occur with conditions such as autism, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, ADHD and post-traumatic stress. post-traumatic stress disorder, mood disorders, learning disorders and dyslexia, Down syndrome, fragile X syndrome, and cerebral palsy.
If your child is having trouble processing sensations, you must first rule out a medical cause. A child who has trouble distinguishing between similar-looking letters, such as P and R, may be farsighted and need glasses.
The therapist may prescribe a sensory diet: a set of activities at home that a child with SPD and their family should engage in on a daily basis to support the development of more adaptive responses to sensations.
