Once thought to not exist, the lymphatic system of our central nervous system is known as the glymphatic system and is made up of water channels, pores, and other cleansing pathways. It is essentially the waste transport system for our central nervous system (brain and spinal cord).
In fact, neurodegenerative diseases are very commonly associated with the accumulation of byproducts of cellular metabolism that are not properly packaged and excreted. These byproducts include proteins, reactive oxygen species, excess ions, and other compounds.
Research has shown that the glymphatic system is most efficient and robust when our hearts pump, our blood flows, our bodies move, and our brains receive regular, restful sleep.
How can we best optimize our glymphatic system, so that it supports all of our detoxification efforts?
Exercise gets our heart pumping and our blood moving. It delivers and exchanges nutrients, oxygen, and metabolic by-products, thus participating in both lymphatic and lymphatic drainage.
This includes the wide range of fruits and vegetables, which contain a multitude of compounds important for combating reactive oxygen species, free radicals and inflammatory mediators. Be sure to include foods rich in magnesium, as magnesium salts contribute to healthy vessel walls.
