Our reality has suddenly changed in a way that we never, in our wildest dreams, thought possible. "Suddenly" is the operative word.
Furthermore, during the same period, a deranged and corrupt president tried to overthrow our democratic electoral process and unleashed a virulent white male supremacy movement. Human beings of other races, genders, or sexualities are increasingly under violent attack, and women's bodies are suddenly under government control.
The most devastating thing is the terrible uncertainty. What will happen with COVID, climate change and the rise of authoritarianism?
Can we make plans for the future?
How to make plans? Will things open up enough to visit the kids or plan a trip abroad?
Of course, such concerns belong only to the most privileged among us. For others, there's job loss, crushing financial anxiety, food insecurity, and working from home with restless kids out of school.
How can we move on?
I firmly believe that the first step is to make an effort to stay awake, aware and in touch with our feelings, no matter how disturbing they may be. Otherwise, we run the risk of dissociating, disconnecting and becoming unconscious.
We must try to live fully. Only then can we do all the things that make us happy: maintaining connections with others, moving our bodies, cultivating positive emotions, staying grounded in the inner sensations of our body1 and always trying to discover what we really want, and doing it!
And we do it better when we have something to look forward to. At this unprecedented moment in history, Barach Obama's words are particularly apt: We need to invoke the audacity of hope.
