However, observation will not be possible if, previously, an adequate definition of the behavior to be modified has not been made. In other words, we are based on the following scheme on the behavior:
Thus, the behavior may be, for example, "crossing the street", but not moving the legs. The behavior has a “meaning”: when I am in front of certain stimuli (street), I move my legs (response), to cross the street (Er +).
Once this has been clarified, we must also affirm, from a less theoretical level (especially, when you talk to other people: parents, teachers ...), the behavior It must be defined in terms that allow it to be measured or, at least, to affirm whether or not they have occurred. For example, it is useless to define that a child is "bad", "crooked", "lazy", "degenerate" ... or, worse still, resorting to "inheritance" or "destiny" to define him: "he is just like his uncle".
For example, observe the difference that makes, when faced with the same event, the following statements: "Pepe is capricious" (Bad: it is not clear why). "If they don't give Pepe sweet for dessert, he throws himself on the ground and kicks" (Well: that's clear).
Another important practical aspect is that good definitions mention what happens in "positive"; that is to say, in things that are done, not in things that are not done. Examples: "Juan must not forget his obligations" "must not be idle" (Bad).
The record of behavior in behavior modification it is essential because if we do not have measures, levels of behavior, we will not be able to later know if it has really been modified.
The choice of units for time (eg, days, hours, months ...) and for thebehavior (total number of behaviors, rate per time ...) depend entirely on the type of problem we are facing; for some will be very frequent and others very infrequent. In addition, the nature of the behavior will tell us what is most appropriate.
There are two basic analyzes in behavior modification: The topographic analysis andFunctional analysis.
Number of behaviors (or variety). Eg Juan jumps, hits his brother, breaks things ... when he doesn't want to go to school
c. Frequency (number of times per unit of time). Eg He gets up 7 times during the meal.
Functional analysis, which is also typical of modifying behavior (and perhaps more defining of it) refers to the functional relationship between antecedent and consequent responses and stimuli. The relationship established with a specific function: ex.
The short-term consequences are those that, in most cases, maintain the behaviors, although the long-term consequences may be negative. Eg we eat a lot because in the short term we like it, although later, in the long term, we regret it ...
