The day before, an 18-year-old killed 19 fourth graders and two teachers. He injured 17 more.
In the month since then, there have been dozens more mass shootings. A renewed conversation about gun control, individual rights, mental health, racism, and violence has gripped our families, communities, and government.
So when these conversations come up, we get defensive. We want to point to all the research that shows that the overwhelming majority of people with mental illnesses, including serious mental illnesses such as psychotic disorders, major depressive disorder, and bipolar disorder, will never commit a violent crime (Swanson, 2021).
We want everyone to understand that when alcohol use, child abuse, unemployment, and underserved neighborhoods are taken into account, there is no additional risk of violence among people diagnosed with mental illness (DeAngelis, 2021). But to stop there would be false.
These fatalities far outnumber those from mass shootings. This complex conversation must also include honesty about policy choices that leave individuals, families, and communities in a constant state of stress and crisis: poor job quality standards, unaffordable medical and mental health care, deserts in the childcare, unsafe environments and lack of weapons.
So far, in focusing the conversation on whether mental health is to blame, we've been dishonest at worst or incomplete at best. It is dishonest for a legislator to talk about the importance of mental health when he has a decades-long voting history of underfunding or underfunding mental health services and other supports needed to thrive.
Here are some actions we can all take to be more honest and act with integrity around mental health and gun violence:
Encourage all people with mental and/or emotional problems to get help from a licensed health professional. This will require familiarity with the resources available in your area.
Support research that explores how to improve the broader contextual factors associated with mental health and gun violence, including poverty and adverse childhood experiences. Be present to people and communities in their complaints about armed violence and recognize the complexities.
Through this honesty, we will be able to better protect our children, neighbors, underserved communities, and those struggling with mental illness.
According to Kaplan, vaginismus is an involuntary spasm of the muscles that surround the vaginal entrance, specifically the sphincter of the vagina and the levator ani, which occurs whenever the introduction of the penis or any object into the vaginal opening is attempted intercourse impossible; This involuntary contraction is a conditioned response that comes from the association of pain or fear with the attempts or even fantasy of vaginal penetration.
The problem focuses on the appearance of a reflex spasm during intercourse that involves the contraction of the muscles at the entrance to the vagina with the consequent closure of the vaginal opening.
Stories of tampax and diaphragm failures and problems during gynecological examinations are also frequent in women with vaginismus.
Other factors are an education that blames sexual relations, fear of pregnancy, previous experience of abuse or rape, fear of pain ...
