Understanding Cognitive Science 4E
4E cognitive science argues that the workings of the mind or the mechanisms of the brain do not exhaust what can be captured about human cognition and behavior. The 4Es headline the fact that cognition is also typically:
Extended by downloading and ordering information in external structures (eg, the coded coordination between a library's catalog and book locations)
Embedded to the extent that concepts are embedded in our bodies, with characteristic configurations and orientations (reflected, for example, in body-based metaphors in ordinary language, such as the feet and head of a bed)
Integrated and extended cognition is primarily concerned with the ways in which context matters. To the extent that the candidate movements and actions that underlie cognitive states depend on the sociocultural context, the represented cognition is also tied to the peculiarities of cultures.
In at least one respect, embodied cognition and the meanings derived from it are not dependent on sociocultural context. Culture has myriad influences on embodiment, but not everything about embodied cognition is culturally contingent.
Embodied cognition demands a 6E cognitive science.
