Psychoanalysis focuses on understanding the unconscious motivations that determine behavior, while behaviorism studies the conditioning processes that produce such behavior. Thus, forerunners of the humanistic psychology they considered that both psychoanalysis and behaviorism were too "pessimistic", since they focused on the most tragic emotions and behaviors or did not take into account the role of personal choice.
Humanism also suggests that people possess personal choice and are motivated to use this free will to pursue the things that will help them reach their full potential as human beings. This need for fulfillment and personal growth is a key motivator for all behavior.
