Self-control —from the perspective of Behavior Modification— consists in the emission, by the subject, of a controlling behavior that will try to alter the probability of occurrence of a conflict behavior (controlled behavior). For example, an obese subject stops buying cupcakes at the supermarket (controlled behavior); A subject who spends too much imposes a route that does not go through the Shopping Center where he buys (controlling behavior).
In principle, as they are self-control behaviors, they should be started without external, physical or social instigation (that is, someone does not have to study because they are being controlled at home). Self-control has nothing to do with "willpower."
