5. Review of applications with the therapist: Once the real situations have been faced, in the therapy sessions the specific applications will be reviewed, the difficulties will be analyzed and a solution will be sought.
Stimulate control techniques:It is about exerting control over the stimuli that are determining the appearance of the problem behavior. Above all, they have been applied to problems such as obesity, where they have also shown great efficacy, and there are also sexual problems, addictive behaviors, lack of study, marital problems, insomnia, etc.
Stimulus removal (Eg, taking the TV out of the study room, not buying hypercaloric products, not carrying loose money so as not to buy tobacco when leaving home, deleting computer games ...).
Change the social environment (eg, exhibitionist only goes through certain places with a friend who prevents the behavior, the housewife goes to the market where the slot machines are with a neighbor ...).
Modify one's physical or physiological conditions (eg, eat before going shopping, practice relaxation before asking your partner for explanations ...).
Work by modules: divide the behaviors into sections leaving gaps between the behaviors, (for example, get ready to go to university: 9:30 am-10am - get up, 10:30-11am - get dressed, 11:30 am-12am - have breakfast ...). So if you have completed a section you can complete the next one, it is so that everything is not annoyed for not being able to do one.
Behavioral programming techniques: Here the consequences of the behavior to be controlled are manipulated and planned. It has the following modalities:
Self-punishment (The subject himself will administer aversive consequences contingently to the realization of a problem behavior). Exs.
Covert methods (The most widely used are covert sensitization - in this particular one a variant is made combining reality with imagination: for example, while an obese subject is actually eating, he imagines how fat he is getting, the consequences on his health , social reproach, etc. - covert self-reinforcement and covert modeling).
Self-control procedures, despite their apparent coldness and image of a man-robot, lead the subjects to feel freer, more secure and with better self-esteem.
As a fundamental problem lies the thorny question of resistance and difficulties for change of many subjects, the issue of the secondary gain of a problem behavior, it is always necessary to understand what role a behavior (problem or not) has in the life of the subject . If this is not taken into account, all training will fail, more than in conventional therapy due to the greater participation of the subject.
Several months ago, I wrote a post about why people stay in unhappy marriages, and judging by reader responses, the struggle between staying or leaving is very real.
Others were frustrated by the financial need to stay with someone despite the state of the marriage.
You can fix your marriage. But it will be painful.
A long time ago, pop culture clung to the idea that it takes 30 days to change a habit. Or maybe it's 28 days?
You have to put your marriage before yourself. And while I'm a big advocate of going to therapy, either alone or with your partner, the reality is that therapy doesn't guarantee that the blush of first love will return, or that the love you give your partner will be given in kind.
And sadly, the better the relationship, the harder it would be to get back. If the start of your courtship had you swooning and swearing you'd found your soul mate, getting back to a romance that sounds worthy of a Disney movie is going to be a much steeper climb than if your relationship had started more slowly and with ups and downs.
You should stay?
Let it go. Because holding on to an argument that doesn't serve your relationship is sending the message that winning an argument is more important than staying in your marriage.
There will be times when the best way to improve a relationship is to simply let go of any arguments, betrayals, or disappointments that are undermining your marriage, and lead with love instead. In other words, in the future, both partners agree that the love they have for each other is a fact, despite the arguments that arise.
But if you always focus on the negatives, it's very easy to forget about the love you have for each other. Leading with love is essentially agreeing to give your partner the benefit of the doubt that they want this relationship to work just as much as you do.
Marriage should not be a well-choreographed dance, but an opportunity to learn and grow with another person.
