I recently received an email from a 22-year-old queer woman recently diagnosed with ADHD and depression.
I'm trying to find someone to talk to. I need help understanding my ADHD.
I want to find someone who can explain to me what is going on in my brain and how I can feel happier. I don't know where to start.
Or you may get feedback from school or work that you're not performing up to par. Maybe you have a hard time making and keeping friends or feel overwhelmed.
You want to choose a therapist who won't judge you or make you feel abnormal, but instead expresses empathy, listens to what you say, pays attention to nonverbal messages, and offers a variety of helpful interventions (cognitive behavioral therapy, internal family systems). , positive psychology, psychodynamic psychotherapy, mindfulness, etc.)
An experienced therapist takes in the whole person: what is going on psychologically, relationally, and in their environment. Instead of asking, “What's wrong with this person?
Either way, when working with an older adolescent or adult with ADHD, the therapist has to do an intricate dance. They must move artfully between examining feelings, thoughts, and behavior while creating interventions with the client that target daily life issues that perpetuate the issues they came in for.
Do you want someone more quiet and introverted or more dynamic and actively engaged?
Informed about autism, learning disabilities, trauma, and cross-cultural issues?
You may want to be able to interview one or two people before making a decision.
Conduct a short phone screening before agreeing to meet: Ask questions like:
“How many clients with ADHD have you worked with and how would you describe your work?”
“Do you coordinate with other professionals who might be involved?” “When and how do you get feedback?”
What types of interventions will you use to help you and/or your child apply and practice what we discussed in the session?
