Give the therapist a chance: The first therapy sessions are to get to know each other and see if they are a good fit. Be prepared to share information about yourself and ask them any questions you may have about them and their work.
Otherwise, if the session is going well with a natural flow of conversation, strong listening, relevant questions, and a positive connection, come back for a second session and begin the therapeutic process.
Trying to get someone to open up when that's the opposite of what they want to do is dangerous. At best, you will annoy them and they may lie to you.
Instead of forcing someone to tell you their secrets, downplay the reasons they need to keep secrets in the first place. When someone decides to hide something, he is protecting himself, like a castle with the drawbridge raised.
If it's the former, then you, something about you, or your involvement in a situation could be the reason they're not opening up. You could be part of the problem or maybe they don't want you to judge them so they keep quiet.
Let them know that you will do your best to stay calm, stay quiet, and listen to the full story. If you know yourself well and there are certain reactions you should avoid, mention those as well.
Remind them that the two of you can talk about the problem slowly over time. Whatever the situation, you can talk about it more than once.
Point out that talking about the problem is not the only option. They can find their own creative way to share what is happening without words.
