To help yourself slow down, mentally count from one to five for each inhalation and do the same for each exhalation.
If, like me, you can get distracted and forget which breath you're taking, a great option is to let your fingers count. One useful method is the "five-finger breath," which is described here:
This practice has been popularized by Dr. Judson Brewer, author of Unwinding Anxiety. The reason it works, he says, is that using your eyes, sense of touch and mind at the same time leaves little room in the brain for anxiety and negative thoughts.
Tie the breath three by five to set the clothes. For example, Stulberg practices it before breakfast, lunch, and dinner.
Teach yourself to use your emotional tension as a trigger to practice the three-by-five breath. Notice that you tense up, stop what you're doing and use the technique.
