As a synthesis, it can be said that many definitions encompass the three aspects: Behavior Modification would be based on learning knowledge, would use a specific experimental methodology and would make use of applied techniques developed within it.
Behavior Modification : Models and currents that use only operant techniques.
That is, Behavior Therapy can be done using operant techniques. Also the reality is that currently it is considered that doing Behavior Therapy is to address only disorders, healing ... (clinical setting) While doing Behavior Modification would be somewhat broader, encompassing all human behaviors (in class, road safety, hygiene, delay mental, sports, business ...).
There are five precursor elements to Behavior Modification:
Studies of human behavior applying a rigorous methodology. Watson and emotional responses in children.
In the XNUMXs there were three areas that worked on Behavior Modification: England, USA and South Africa.
Eysenck works in England, his first texts analyze the effectiveness of different intervention methods, comparing results. In 1959 he began his work in Behavioral Therapy: application of learning theories in therapeutic intervention.
The Behavior Modification is delimited and explained in accordance with some requirements that we are now going to present numbered. Of course, there are some of the points mentioned now that do not fully match some of the current trends in Behavior Modification, but, in general, they are the aspects that best frame it.
That is, there is first a theoretical development that produces models that explain behaviors and, from these developments, treatments that are experimentally contrasted are proposed. Normal and abnormal behavior are not qualitatively different.
We work in the here and now: current conditions. The goal of Behavior Modification is to change or eliminate maladaptive behavior by substituting it for another and / or teach adapted behavior when it does not occur.
The treatment is adapted to the case: each subject (or each group of subjects) is a case.
This does not imply that the events of the past are unimportant (they are a learning history), but that the conditions responsible for the initial development of the disorder may not be the same ones that currently maintain the problem. The effort to alter past situations must be considered futile, since only present or future events are susceptible to change.
It should also be noted that all the techniques used in behavior therapy must have been proven in a multitude of subjects in controlled studies. For example, extinction or time-out procedures are first tried (like medications) in different cases and then used for a specific child's problem.
In this model there is, therefore, a methodological logic: it starts from a conception of the problem behavior (as an exacerbation of the normal one) and corrective practices are derived from that conception, corrective practices (or techniques) that are experimentally demonstrated before put them into practice.
