Above all, you miss the real opportunity to figure out what the problem or concern is, how to fix it, and where the responsibility lies. This does not happen when you choose to blame rather than discuss the situation.
It can be defensive: the culprit is struggling with something and is taking it out on their partner.
The culprit has a hidden resentment towards his partner.
The blamer learned it from his parents who were blamers.
It certainly didn't come with our wedding vows; they were all about promising to love, honor, cherish, have and keep, etc. When you indulge in blame, you have forgotten that your partner is an individual in their own right.
Her spouse had agreed to tidy up the kitchen for guests who were coming over for dinner one night, but didn't. Are you angry.
Your anger is fast and without time to think, you fall into the mental error of automatically attributing the action of your partner (the non-action in this case) to his character or personality trait of "irresponsibility".
“Taking something personally” happens when an event means something special to you, something that only you can identify. In the example above, it may have been from looking good for the company, feeling unconcerned about him/her doing the assigned task, feeling overwhelmed with household chores, etc.
When we automatically react with emotions such as anger, fear, and/or pain, we are likely to make what social scientists call the "fundamental attribution error." because of something about them, for example their personality traits, rather than their circumstances, such as their partner being rushed with other tasks and unable to get to them.
A note on your automatic emotions: Psychiatrist David Viscott describes the ways we talk about these "rapid response" emotions. Fear can be expressed as being scared, nervous, nervous, having cold feet, nervous, worried, insecure, tense, and trembling.
More on the fundamental attribution error: Keep in mind that we humans tend to think that other people act because of their unique personality traits, temperament, or individual characteristics. This is particularly true when we get angry, scared and/or hurt, when we take things personally.
Your partner, the "culprit", will become a "blamer" and a conflict will ensue.
It precludes any chance to discuss the situation, find out what happened, and rectify the situation.
If you want to stop using blame as a way to hold your spouse accountable, you need to increase your "emotional intelligence" by paying attention when you're angry, upset, or hurt by something your spouse has done that you don't like.
Being self-reflective allows you to avoid the mental error of automatically assuming that what happened was due to some negative trait in your spouse. Now you understand that we are all at risk of making this kind of mental error when we are upset with our spouses.
