A properly placed broken bone heals in six weeks, but a brain damaged by verbal or psychological abuse may not heal properly or even be recognized as injured. If a child is raped, the law treats it as a serious crime.
Is the adult someone to be feared or trusted, someone who is healthy or sick, an individual who protects or exploits, a guide or a pedophile? The brain cannot predict.
Legislators, teachers, and parents need to be aware of the serious harm that results from any form of emotional neglect and psychological abuse. Even if it is not yet reflected in the law, they need to know that just because the body is not the target, the brain is vulnerable to harm.
The emotionally neglected child does not have a healthy, high-performing brain. Children and youth need to learn to develop positive, trusting, and connected relationships with adults and peers, and this learning depends on adults providing positive and healthy care.
Help your child learn to collaborate.
In a new study of more than 11,200 American children ages 9 to 13, researchers found that kids who played team sports like basketball and soccer were less likely to have signs of anxiety, depression, isolation, social problems, and problems of attention. Compared with kids who didn't play a team sport, team players had 17 percent less social difficulty on measures based on parent responses.
The players on the team may have had fewer social difficulties because they had practiced cognitive empathy - understanding how others think. Many competitive activities require youth to anticipate what others may do in response to their actions.
To compete well over time, you must recognize your strengths and weaknesses and those of your teammates or rivals.
Help your child find role models.
We know that Lebron James is a basketball expert because of his ridiculously high number of shots, rebounds, blocks, and ultimately wins. Without the competition to showcase his abilities, would our students still be able to recognize him as the hero they aspire to?
Help your child celebrate success at all stages.
Teach your child to look for growth.
Along the way, your child should ideally learn that it's okay to ask for help and take advice. It becomes clear that other people can help you do better next time or stretch yourself to the next level.
