Less than 1 percent of Americans are sensitive to gluten. Many of these individuals have celiac disease.
Today, due to fears about the possible negative impact of gluten, around 1,6 million follow a gluten-free diet. This is surprising given that most of these people probably do not have gluten sensitivity.
Many books and websites also claim that gluten causes unwanted weight gain. A recent study investigated this claim and concluded that there is no evidence that gluten peptides influence the brain's appetite control centers or alter the body's energy expenditure.
Essentially, the current scientific and clinical literature concludes that if you are not sensitive to gluten, eating gluten-containing foods is beneficial to your health. A recent study showed that dietary gluten does not increase the risk of inflammatory bowel disorder in adults who do not have celiac disease.
The authors warned that avoiding gluten could increase the risk of cardiovascular disease due to reduced consumption of beneficial whole grains. Whole grains are naturally high in fiber and can reduce the risk of heart disease, diabetes, certain cancers, and other health problems.
