Social phobia is a disorder that affects a large number of people, being one of the most common mental illnesses among the population worldwide. This type of anxiety disorder can be detected by signs of extreme shame, great insecurity, great discomfort when speaking in public, and so on.
People with this disorder are aware that they should not feel bad about these situations, but they are not able to control their anxiety and fear when they occur. Also, on a regular basis, they try to avoid situations in which they may feel discomfort.
Despite the fact that some studies assure that social phobia may be caused by a neurotransmitter mismatch, the most common causative factors of this type of disorder are traumatic experiences lived in the past, the limitation of opportunities to interact at a social level or the family overprotection during growth.
