BlogDePsychology defines resilience as “the psychological quality that allows some people to be knocked down by the adversities of life and come back at least as strong as before”. adversities of life
No matter how hard we try, at some point in our lives, we will find ourselves navigating painful and challenging situations, some of our own making, many beyond our control, and many that can throw us off course and threaten to derail our lives. .
We all have days when something throws us off course. Sometimes we find that our usual approach to completing a task is no longer effective.
It is often said that life does not give us more than we can handle. Whether or not we accept this notion, when faced with an obstacle, we will learn from it before it is gone.
Resilience is an evidence-based inference that some people do better than others who have experienced a comparable level of adversity; in addition, the negative experience may have a sensitizing effect or a strengthening effect in relation to the response to subsequent stress or adversity.
Over 15 years of study, my colleagues and I discovered that the answer is optimism.
We can imagine catastrophic events unfolding as worst case scenarios. We may doubt our ability to cope with loss, failure, or an unforeseen tragedy.
Anyone who has ever experienced adversity has one thing in common: they did the best they could under the circumstances they encountered. When life presented them with unforeseeable and unimaginable difficult situations, they did their best with the knowledge and skills available to them at the time.
Seven steps to building resilience
Remember that you have handled adversity in your life to the best of your ability. Reframe negative events.
Develop a meditation practice, whether that means sitting quietly or immersing yourself in an activity or hobby you enjoy. A meditation practice allows you to calm your mind and connect with your inner strength.
