The Implications of Poverty, Neglect, and Medication for Childhood Trauma Care
Poverty is, if not the main factor, why we have too many traumatized children. Children living in poverty or low-income settings and others in Child Protective Services (CPS) or foster care are more likely to experience one or more forms of childhood trauma.
Children living in poverty and low-income communities are more likely to experience trauma in the form of exposure to community violence, domestic violence, personal/interpersonal violence, and substance abuse. If a child lives in a home with substance abuse or domestic violence, or living in poverty creates toxic stress for the family, the youth may not feel cared for, appreciated, or loved either.
But for those living in poverty, those children, by default, are experiencing conditions of poverty. There is also a greater chance that their schools and routes to school are not as safe as they deserve to be, and as a result, they may be bullied or witness school violence more often.
Therefore, most children in CPS supervision and foster care are typically exposed to the same levels of traumatic conditions and events as children in poor and low-income communities. And for children who are taken from their homes to be placed in foster care (experience forced displacement), we add another traumatic experience.
Reflecting on the signs of trauma that I shared in Part 1 of this series, let us think about the implications that such understandable and justifiable reactions to trauma have on education and the challenges it brings to educators trying to achieve a moment of Zen and make test scores are miraculous. achieve proficiency levels.
These signs and symptoms are very likely to prevent students from concentrating or finding lessons of interest. And if children go untreated for such trauma, how can we expect educators to be able to teach populations of children who have been traumatized?
What are case and social workers supposed to do when they have limited time due to high caseloads?
In many states, my child welfare team applying a VitalChild-VitalClient-centric lens has been told that due to limited service providers, it could take months for a child to get an appointment with a therapist, doctor, or counselor. This is due to provider shortages and client backlogs, and is further challenged by outdated assessments and technology-driven processes to accelerate more efficient care.
Additionally, due to a misguided ethical belief that mental health professionals should endorse a client's medication when a mental disorder is identified, many are adopting a medication-guided, symptom-by-symptom approach to treatment. Too many mental health professionals endorse or prescribe a collection of powerful brain-numbing and mind-controlling drugs to mask or minimize any and all symptoms of a disorder a young person is hypothesized to have.
How can we label the basic and justifiable reactions to the challenges of a traumatic conflict life as signs of a latent mental disorder?
And these "side effects," such as suicidal ideation, worse depression, OCD tendencies, drug addiction, and even overdose and death, are often worse than the original symptoms being medicated. When one shows more symptoms due to the accompanying side effects of these psychotropics, it can unfortunately lead to a diagnosis of more mental disorders and more medications, a vicious cycle.
Imagine trying to balance a large water balloon in your hands. Leaning on one end only displaces the water to the other side, while you struggle to contain it.
Shared goals: Do you have shared goals and also support and celebrate each other's personal goals?
According to research in psychology, one of the most important predictors of a healthy relationship is being emotionally responsive (Lemay et al., 2007). This involves sending signals (eg, verbal, physical) to your partner and having them respond to them (eg, calming, encouraging).
Being vulnerable with others. When partners open up to each other, it helps to develop and strengthen mutual trust.
This can go hand in hand with vulnerability, but also encompasses other forms of communication. A healthy relationship is not likely to be based on lies.
Have "healthy" conflicts. Conflicts are inevitable in any relationship, but how you deal with them is essential.
Try something new. This is especially helpful if your relationship is feeling stuck and it can rekindle the spark (for example, going to a new restaurant for a date night).
Solve problems as a team. This can help strengthen your identity as a "we" rather than a "me" and a "you" and build your problem-solving skills together (for example, this can range from an escape room to asking your partner for help with a problem at work).
Adapted from an article published by The Berkeley Well-Being Institute.
The modality of Distance psychology is available to all people who, for work reasons, travel or work They can't go to a doctor's office. This type of care has several advantages in favor of helping the patient.
How does online psychology therapy help?
Online psychological therapy favors many aspects in the life of a person who is going through different situations. The most effective way to check its advantages is to know them and try them.
If you need more privacy in each session, you can do them from the comfort of your home or a place where you feel less stressful and your confidence increases as the therapy progresses.
If you prefer to be attended by a professional who is not related to your local environment or circle of acquaintances, and anonymity is not your thing, you can go to a specialist outside your area. You can shorten the distance and access institutions outside your region for more reliable regular sessions.
Starting psychological therapy is a decision that is not taken lightly, but it is a step that yields positive results and helps improve the lives of many people. There are many advantages that also stand out in online therapy.
It gives you the opportunity to clarify your thoughts, behaviors and emotions.
In each session your confidence can increase considerably.
you achieve a higher level of awareness and sense of control about your life.
Although in these types of situations it may be thought that it is difficult to overcome those circumstances and that it seems that they will remain in our being permanently, you must bear in mind that the human being is capable of overcoming what has done so much damage, but for this you must make use of the appropriate tools.
This treatment is the most innovative to be able to apply it when treating thepost traumatic stress disorder, thus allowing the affected people to overcome those psychological traumas that they may suffer much faster than following other traditional therapies.
This treatment has been developed for more than twenty years by professionals, with a high success rate when it comes to solving those traumas that have to do with terrorism, rape, victims of violence, natural disasters, accidents ... However, it is not only apply thisEMDR technique For these extraordinary cases, it is also used to solve other problems such as addictions, emotional or work disappointments, phobias, or the loss of loved ones, among others.
To achieve this, it focuses on what the person has and not on what is lacking, based on the ability of the human being innately to be able to live his life in the best possible way through the search for the resources that are necessary for it. This tendency to grow naturally can be achieved through a meeting with a professional who uses this technique, who is thus able to create a bond that makes threats aside and may have acceptance, authenticity and empathy.
These professionals are in charge of giving therapy to those people who come to their consultation for family or individual problems, being able to advise on duels, divorces, family conflicts, vital crises, etc. It also includes those who seek the resolution of conflicts related to the work or institutional environment, so its field of action is very high.
It should be taken into account that despite the fact that all those traumatic circumstances that we suffer in life leave traces that are indelible in our mind and our interior, the reality is that no problem can be considered to be definitive and permanent, since that thanks to the appropriate therapy carried out by duly qualified professionals, it is possible to leave behind such painful episodes and thus be able to rebuild our lives to achieve the goal of being happy.
